 "next_text": "Tomorrow, we open another forgotten page of history, and we follow the people who lived it.",
 "text_sent": "They pulled a burned body from the palace and called it the emperor. So why did his uncle keep searching for him? The emperor was Jianwen, the young grandson of the man who founded China's Ming dynasty. His uncles were powerful princes, and the strongest, Zhu Di, guarded the north. When Jianwen began stripping his uncles of their power, Zhu Di rebelled. After three years of war, his army reached Nanjing, the capital, and a general opened a gate to him. Then the palace caught fire. Zhu Di's men brought a body out of the flames, and he buried it as the emperor. Jianwen's eldest son, a small boy, was never seen again. Zhu Di took the throne as the Yongle Emperor, and wiped his nephew's reign from the calendar. But a rumour spread that Jianwen had escaped. Yongle sent an official to search the empire for him, year after year. The official History of Ming says the treasure fleets sailed partly to find him. Most historians doubt it. More than twenty years after the fire, that official came back, and Yongle rose from bed to talk with him deep into the night. Only then, the history says, was Yongle's suspicion laid to rest. It never says what he was told. Whether Jianwen died in that fire, or walked out of it, no one has ever proved. <break time=\"0.8s\"/> History remembers the victors. We remember the rest.",
